```java
public record AuditEvent(
    String actorId,
    String action,
    String targetId,
    Instant occurredAt,
    Map<String, Object> details
) {}

public interface AuditLogger {
    void record(AuditEvent event);
}
```

One method, one job. `record()` takes a fully-formed, immutable event — there's no `update()` or `delete()` on this interface, which is itself the design statement. Compare this to [designing public APIs](designing-public-apis): the absence of a method is as much a design decision as its presence.

```java
public class FileAuditLogger implements AuditLogger {
    public void record(AuditEvent event) {
        appendLine(serialize(event));
    }
    // no update(id, ...) or delete(id) — intentionally absent
}
```

The implementation only ever appends — no code path seeks back into the file or store and rewrites a prior record. If a later requirement demands "correcting" an entry, the fix is a **new compensating event** ("correction: X was actually Y"), never a mutation of the original, so the trail of what happened — including the correction — stays intact.
